\begin{center}
    \emph{What are the Betti numbers $\beta_r$ of $K$?}
\end{center}
Several prior works~\cite{lloyd2016quantum, berry2024analyzing, hayakawa2022quantum, mcardle2022streamlined}, grounded in homology theory, have addressed this question under the assumption that access is provided via an oracle encoding pairwise connectivity among the data points. Although a rigorous treatment of the relevant algebraic topology will be provided in~\cref{sec: reviewalgebraictopology}, we briefly review several essential concepts to facilitate the exposition of our main results.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item An $r$-simplex is a fully connected subgraph on $(r{+}1)$ vertices (see~\cref{fig: simplex} for an illustration). Thus, a 0-simplex is a vertex, a 1-simplex is an edge, and a 2-simplex is a triangle, and so on.
    \item A simplicial complex is a collection of simplices of various orders satisfying specific closure properties.
    \item A (formal) linear combination of $r$-simplices is referred to as an $r$-chain.
    \item The boundary operator $\partial_r$ maps an $r$-simplex to a (formal) linear combination of $(r{-}1)$-simplices.
    \item An $r$-cycle is an $r$-chain $c_r$ satisfying $\partial_r c_r = 0$.
\end{enumerate}

Within homology theory, the set of $r$-simplices in $K$, denoted by $S_r^K = \{ \sigma_{r_i} \}_{i=1}^{|S_r^K|}$, spans a vector space (alternatively, one may work with a finitely generated abelian group). An $r$-chain can then be represented as a vector in this space, and the boundary operator $\partial_r$ acts as a linear map between the corresponding vector spaces. Given the family of boundary maps $\{ \partial_r \}$, one can define the so-called $r$-th \emph{combinatorial Laplacian} as
\begin{equation}\label{eq: r_th_comb_laplacian}
    \Delta_r = \partial_{r+1} \partial_{r+1}^\dagger + \partial_r^\dagger \partial_r.
\end{equation}
The $r$-th Betti number $\beta_r$ is given by the dimension of the kernel of $\Delta_r$, i.e., $\dim \ker (\Delta_r) = \beta_r$.   

Given access to the aforementioned oracle, it is possible to construct $\Delta_r$ in a quantum manner and analyze its kernel, for instance, via the phase estimation algorithm, as demonstrated in~\cite{lloyd2016quantum}. A more detailed review of previous quantum algorithms~\cite{lloyd2016quantum, berry2024analyzing, hayakawa2022quantum, mcardle2022streamlined} for estimating Betti numbers is provided in~\cref{fig: lgz-flow} and~\cref{sec: TDA}.

Despite these promising developments, a recent result by Schmidhuber and Lloyd~\cite{schmidhuber2022complexity} has established that computing Betti numbers is {\#P}-hard, and even estimating them to within a given precision is {NP}-hard. This result effectively rules out the possibility of achieving an unconditional quantum advantage under such oracle-based input assumptions. As emphasized in~\cite{schmidhuber2022complexity}, it is therefore crucial to explore alternative means of specifying simplicial complexes, beyond reliance solely on pairwise connectivity.

\subsection{Input model setup}
Motivated by this insight, we propose a modified input assumption. Let $S_r^K$ denote the set of $r$-simplices in $K$, so that $K = \{ S_r^K \}_{r=1}^n$, where $n$ denotes the number of data points. We assume that a \emph{classical description} of $K$ is provided. By this, we mean that for each $r$, we are given classical access to a matrix $\mathcal{S}_r$ of size $|{S_{r-1}^K}| \times |{S_r^K}|$. Each column $i$ of $\mathcal{S}_r$, corresponding to an $r$-simplex $\sigma_{r_i}$, contains nonzero entries equal to $1$, and the indices of these entries indicate the $(r{-}1)$-simplices that are faces of $\sigma_{r_i}$. Equivalently, we may restate the problem as follows:
\begin{problem}[Betti number estimation with classical description, see~\cref{sec: estimatingBettinumbers}]\label{prob: betti_classical}
Given a simplicial complex $K$, specified via a classical description of its boundary matrices $\{ \mathcal{S}_r \}$, estimate the Betti numbers $\{ \beta_r \}$ of $K$. This formulation contrasts with prior oracle-based models; here, the simplicial complex is assumed to be explicitly given in classical form.
\end{problem}

\subsection{Estimating Betti numbers}
Given the matrices $\{ \mathcal{S}_r \}$, one can efficiently recover the classical descriptions of the boundary maps $\{ \partial_r \}$; see~\cref{sec: reviewalgebraictopology} for formal definitions. The overall procedure is summarized in~\cref{fig: betti_workflow}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\DontPrintSemicolon
\textbf{Input:} Classical description of a simplicial complex $K = \{ S_r^K \}_{r=1}^n$

Construct boundary maps $\partial_r$ from $K$ %\tcp*{Classical preprocessing}

Compute a block-encoding of $\partial_r^\dagger \partial_r / ((r+1)|S_r^K|)$ %\tcp*{Lemma~\cref{lemma: entrycomputablematrix}}

Construct a block-encoding of the combinatorial Laplacian $\Delta_r / (2(r+1)(r+2)|S_r^K||S_{r+1}^K|)$ %\tcp*{Lemmas~\cref{lemma: sumencoding},~\cref{lemma: scale}} 

Estimate $\operatorname{rank}(\Delta_r)/|S_r^K|$ using stochastic rank estimation %\tcp*{Lemma~\cref{lemma: stochasticrankestimation}}

Compute $\beta_r / |S_r^K| = 1 - \operatorname{rank}(\Delta_r)/|S_r^K|$

\textbf{Output:} Normalized Betti number $\beta_r / |S_r^K|$
\caption{\justifying Quantum algorithm for estimating normalized Betti numbers}
\label{fig: betti_workflow}
\end{algorithm}

We define the quantity
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\dim\ker(\Delta_r)}{|S_r^K|} = \frac{\beta_r}{|{S_r^K}|}
\end{equation}
as the \emph{$r$-th normalized Betti number}. The algorithm described above yields the following result:

\begin{theorem}[Time complexity of estimating (normalized) Betti numbers, see~\cref{sec: estimatingBettinumbers}]
\label{thm: estimatingbettinumbers}
Let $\{ \mathcal{S}_r \}$ be the classical specification of a simplicial complex $K$. Then, the $r$-th normalized Betti number $\beta_r / |S_r^K|$ can be estimated to additive precision $\epsilon$ with time complexity
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\log(r|{S_r^K}|) \log(r^2 |{S_r^K}||{S_{r+1}^K}|)}{\epsilon^2}\right).
\end{equation}
Moreover, the $r$-th Betti number $\beta_r$ can be estimated to multiplicative precision $\delta$ with time complexity
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\log (r|{S_r^K}|) \log(r^2 |{S_r^K}||{S_{r+1}^K}|)|{S_r^K}|^2}{\delta^2 \beta_r^2}\right).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

As we summarize in~\cref{sec: TDA}, to the best of our knowledge, the state-of-the-art quantum algorithm (referred to as the LGZ algorithm) achieves the following time complexities for estimating normalized Betti numbers (to additive precision $\epsilon$) and unnormalized Betti numbers (to multiplicative precision $\delta$), respectively:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O} \left(\frac{1}{\epsilon}\cdot\left(n^2 \sqrt{\frac{\binom{n}{r+1}}{|{S_r^K}|}} + n\kappa\right)\right), \quad
    \mathcal{O} \left(\frac{1}{\delta} \cdot \left( n^2 \sqrt{\frac{\binom{n}{r+1}}{\beta_r}} + n\kappa \sqrt{\frac{|{S_r^K}|}{\beta_r}} \right) \right),
\end{equation}
where $\kappa$ denotes the condition number of the combinatorial Laplacian $\Delta_r$.

As can be seen, with respect to the normalized Betti numbers $\beta_r / |S_r^K|$, the LGZ algorithm is efficient only when $|S_r^K|$ is sufficiently large; that is, when the simplicial complex is dense. In the best-case scenario where the ratio $\binom{n}{r+1} / |S_r^K|$ is $\mathcal{O}(1)$, the LGZ algorithm achieves time complexity $\mathcal{O}(n^2 + n\kappa)$.

In contrast, as long as $|S_r^K| \in \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{poly}(n))$, our algorithm achieves time complexity $\mathcal{O}(\mathrm{poly}(\log (n)))$, thus exhibiting a \emph{superpolynomial} speed-up over the LGZ algorithm. The advantage becomes even more pronounced when the ratio $\binom{n}{r+1} / |S_r^K|$ is itself in $\mathcal{O}(\mathrm{poly}(n))$.

In the \emph{sparse regime}, where $|S_r^K| \ll \binom{n}{r+1}$, the ratio 
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\binom{n}{r+1}}{|S_r^K|} \approx \binom{n}{r+1},    
\end{equation}
and the LGZ algorithm suffers from large overhead. Meanwhile, the classical specification cost in our approach remains $\mathcal{O} \left( \log (r |S_r^K|) \cdot \log(r^2 |S_r^K| |S_{r+1}^K|) \right)$, which is significantly smaller than
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}\left( \log\left(r \binom{n}{r+1}\right) \cdot \log\left(r^2 \binom{n}{r+1} \binom{n}{r+2}\right) \right).    
\end{equation}
Therefore, if $\binom{n}{r+1} \in \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{poly}(n))$, our algorithm achieves a superpolynomial speed-up. If $\binom{n}{r+1} \in \exp(n)$, the resulting speed-up becomes \emph{nearly exponential}.

In terms of estimating the (unnormalized) Betti numbers $\beta_r$, both algorithms achieve their best performance when $\beta_r \approx |S_r^K|$, i.e., when the normalized Betti number is constant. In this regime, our algorithm exhibits time complexity $\mathcal{O}\left( \log (r |S_r^K| ) \cdot \log ( r^2 |S_r^K| |S_{r+1}^K| ) \right)$, while the LGZ algorithm yields
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}\left( n^2 \sqrt{\frac{\binom{n}{r+1}}{\beta_r}} \right) \approx \mathcal{O}\left( n^2 \sqrt{\frac{\binom{n}{r+1}}{|S_r^K|}} \right).    
\end{equation}
Thus, the comparison remains similar: our algorithm achieves a superpolynomial speed-up in the dense regime and a near-exponential speed-up in the sparse regime.

