In order to use the fluctuations in the canonical variables to compute the fluctuations in other systems of parameters, we employ the method used in standard error propagation. Thus, for any given center of mass or relative parameter $\chi$,
\begin{equation}
\expval{\Delta \chi^2} = \expval{\qty[\sum_k \pdv{\chi}{\xi_k}\delta \xi_k]^2},
\label{errorprop}
\end{equation}
where each $\xi_k$ is one of the canonical variables $q_i,p_i,\rho_i,$ or $\phi_i$. For example, the initial fluctuations in the total number of atoms $N$ are calculated by
\begin{equation}
    \expval{\Delta N^2} = \frac{1}{\hbar^2}\qty(\expval{\delta \rho_1^2}+2\expval{\delta \rho_1\delta \rho_2}+\expval{\delta \rho_2^2}) \, .
\end{equation}
Using the values reported in Table \ref{table:results_2sol_canonical} and relationships in Table \ref{table:COMCanonical} and \eqref{errorprop}, we obtain an exact reproduction of the results summarized in Table I in \cite{marchukov2020} with $N=1$.

