\filldraw [fill=gray, draw=black] (7,1.25) circle (0.1);
\filldraw [fill=gray, draw=black] (8,1.25) circle (0.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\hspace{2mm}
\begin{tikzpicture} [scale=0.2]
\draw (0.0,0.0) -- (2.47,0.0) -- (2.47,2.5) -- (0.0,2.5);
\filldraw [fill=gray, draw=black] (0.9,1.8) circle (0.7);
\filldraw [fill=gray, draw=black] (1.77,0.7) circle (0.7);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{equation}
representing the state with the highest number of disks per unit length overall.
The categories remain the same: compacts $m=1,2$, hosts $m=3,\ldots,14$ and tags $m=15,16,17$. 
Most particles are again stable.
Particles $5,6$ are unstable, whereas particles $1,2,15,16$ in some configurations are only marginally stable.
Ways to retain only the stable configurations will be implemented in Sec.~\ref{sec:scen2}.
We shall demonstrate that the main conclusions are not affected by these instances of marginal stability or instability.

