\section{Integrating the Symbolic and Control Layers}

Consider now the robotic aide described in the Introduction of the manuscript. Here we describe an integration of the low level control operation with the higher level conceptual discrete planning and scheduling decision making.

Figure~\ref{fig:schema} presents the high level picture of the operation. Over a long, slow time scale, a Scheduler decides upon a sequence of tasks. When the time comes for each task, a planning problem corresponding to that task is formulated and solved. In the course of executing the solution of the planning problem, each action taken generates a continuous control problem that is solved at a fast time scale that yields the true reward and next state, which may or may not be the one predicted by the planner. Models of rewards and transitions are learned, with information flowing across layers in a continuous, sequential manner.
\begin{figure}
    \includegraphics[scale=0.45]{schema.png}
    \caption{A pictorial model of integrating the Symbolic Planning and Control Layers in Operation. The scheduler decides upon the sequence of Tasks the robot is set to perform. Each Task defines a Planning Problem. The solution to the problem is computed, and then at each time step, for a given state an action is taken which then defines an MPC problem. The MPC problem is solved in real time, and the final (continuous) state is mapped to the set of (discrete) states in the planning ontology. Discrepancies in the form of intermediate rewards and unexpected state transitions can change the planning problem in real-time, necessitating a re-computation of the planning solution.}\label{fig:schema}
\end{figure}


