\section{Introduction}
Over the course of their studies, students of physics accumulate experience in solving problems for a variety forces, not only developing a strong physical intuition for each force but also having an understanding of how each force is realized in the physical world. With this in mind we believe not enough emphasis is placed on the reciprocal velocity force $F(v)=\frac{C}{v}$.\footnote{We restrict ourselves to one dimension and take $F(v)$ to be the component of the force.} The theoretical importance of this force is illustrated by the units of the constant $C$ which are power. Such a force has constant power as $P(t)=F(v(t))v(t)=C$. The goal of this paper is to use graphical methods to explain how such a peculiar force\footnote{This is a velocity dependent force that decreases with increasing $v$, and is even singular at $v=0$, which is quite different from air resistance.} arises from a device called a transmission (Sec. \ref{graphicalInterpretation}), to explore some of the consequences of this force (Sec. \ref{ConstantPower}), and to briefly discuss its role in automotive physics (Sec. \ref{Automotive}).

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


\section{Transmissions} \label{graphicalInterpretation}

Suppose we are given a device that can produce the force $F(v)$ at speed $v$, but are unsatisfied with this force function and would like to transform it into a different one, but without the use of an energy source to do so. If we connect it to the input port of a simple machine of mechanical advantage $N$, it will produce a force $\tilde{F}=N F(v)$ at speed $\tilde{v}=v/N$ at the output port. Expressing the output force $\tilde{F}$ as a function of the output speed $\tilde{v}$ we get

\begin{equation} \label{transformeqn}
    \tilde{F}(\tilde{v})=N F (N\tilde{v}).
\end{equation}


Fig. \ref{constEngineForce} gives an example of an input force function that has a limited range punctuated with a sharp velocity cutoff. The output force function for a variety of values of $N$ are given in Fig. \ref{constTransmissionForce}. $N>1$ increases the force while decreasing the range while $N<1$ decreases the force while increasing the range.\footnote{This must be true as the transformation preserves the area: $\int_0^\infty Nf(Nx)dx =\int_0^\infty f(x) dx$.}\\

A transmission is a device that allows $N=N(\tilde{v})$. By having such freedom a variety of transformations are possible. For example the selection

\begin{align}
N_{dis}(\tilde{v})=
\begin{cases}
    3, &\tilde{v}<10\\
    2, &10\leq \tilde{v}<15\\
    1, &15 \leq \tilde{v}<30\\
    1/2, &\tilde{v}\geq 30
\end{cases}
\end{align}

creates the staircase-shaped force function shown in Fig. \ref{constComboForce}. When $N(\tilde{v})$ is piecewise constant like this we call each different piece a selected gear. \\

