\section{Constant Power} \label{ConstantPower}

We now focus on constant power. Integrating $P=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{1}{2}m v^2 \right)$ one gets 

\begin{equation} \label{constPowEqn2}
    v=\sqrt{v_i^2+\frac{2P}{m}(t-t_i)}.
\end{equation}

Differentiating and integrating this expression gives
\begin{align}\label{constPowEqn3}
    a&=\frac{P}{m}\sqrt{\frac{1}{v_i^2+\frac{2P}{m}(t-t_i)}} \\ \nonumber
    x&=\frac{m}{3P}\left(v_i^2+\frac{2P}{m}(t-t_i)\right)^\frac{3}{2}+\left(x_i-\frac{m}{3P}v_i^3\right).
\end{align}

\begin{comment}
    The initial values $(t_i,x_i,v_i)$ are determined by a different force.
\end{comment}

 The character of the $P/v$ force is better understood by comparing it to the constant force at very large times. The result is summarized in Tbl. \ref{forceComparison}. We note that although the power is not increasing with time, the velocity still becomes unbounded as the object is always being fed a constant stream of energy. If however we include air resistance a maximum velocity will instead be reached:

\begin{align}
\frac{P}{v_{max}}&- \gamma v_{max}^2=0\\ \nonumber
v_{max}&=\sqrt[3]{\frac{P}{\gamma}}.
\end{align}

This maximum velocity, the result of a balance of forces, does not depend on the mass, whereas the kinematic variables in Tbl. \ref{forceComparison} do through the $P/m$ ratio.

