A motor is a device that takes the current $I(t)$ passing through it and converts it to a torque $\tau(t)=N I(t)$ for some constant $N$, but from the viewpoint of the circuit acts as a negative battery with a voltage proportional to its speed: $\Delta V_{motor}=-k \omega$ for some constant $k$. From Kirchoff's rules we have $V-IR-k \omega=0$ and substituting torque for current gives:

\begin{align}
\tau&=\frac{VN}{R}-\left(\frac{kN}{R}\right)\omega \\ \nonumber
&=\tau_{stall}-\left(\frac{\tau_{stall}}{\omega_{free}}\right)\omega 
\end{align}

The torque-speed curve for a motor then has a linear decrease of acceleration rather than the inverse velocity $1/\omega$. Some authors have advocated using a linear decrease of acceleration to model internal combustion engines. From a theoretical perspective the $P/\omega$ decrease is more elegant as it only involves a single parameter $P$, but from a modeling perspective the linear decrease with its two parameters has more freedom to fit data. As electric vehicles become more popular it would make sense to fit internal combustion engine data to their torque output to facilitate cross comparison. Although the resulting equations of motion for racing would take calculus to solve:

\begin{align}
\tau_{stall}-\left(\frac{\tau_{stall}}{\omega_{free}}\right)\omega = J \frac{d\omega }{dt}
\end{align}

where $J$ is the moment of inertia of the wheels we note that this is the equation for the charging of a capacitor where instead of charge $Q$ we have velocity $\omega$.\footnote{A freely spinning motor can be seen as a capacitor that stores angular velocity instead of charge. If the battery were to be replaced with a resistor, then the freely spinning motor would act as a generator and discharge its store energy until it ran out of velocity and the equations would mirror the discharge of a capacitor, suggestive of regernative braking.}, so borrowing the solution we get:

\begin{equation}
    w(t)=\omega_{free}+\left(\omega_i-\omega_{free}\right) e^{-\left(\frac{\tau_{stall}}{J\omega_{free}}\right)(t-t_i)}
\end{equation}

