\title{Analytical solution of coupled self--consistency and linearised Usadel equations for the dirty finite--sized superconductor at critical temperature}


\author{S.S. Seidov}
\affiliation{HSE University, Moscow, Russia}
\author{N.G. Pugach}
\affiliation{HSE University, Moscow, Russia}

\begin{abstract}
In this manuscript we analytically investigate the linearised Usadel equation and the self--consistency equation, defining the critical temperature of the superconducting phase transition of a finite--sized superconductor in the dirty limit. This is a system of coupled differential and integral equations for the anomalous Green function and the order parameter of the superconductor. The formal solution of the system is found for the general case of the linearised boundary conditions, reducing the solution to the solution of an eigenvalue problem. The latter defines the critical temperature of the superconducting phase transition and the spatial distributions of the anomalous Green function and the order parameter.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Finite--sized dirty superconductors present significant interest from both fundamental and practical points of view. When the superconductor is brought in contact with different materials, the properties of electronic subsystems both in the superconductor and the second material are changed due to mutual influence --- this is the so--called proximity effect \cite{de_gennes_boundary_1964, buzdin_proximity_2005}. This allows to create uncommon electronic states in both materials, such as triplet electron pairing \cite{bergeret_odd_2005, eschrig_general_2015}, topological states \cite{Leijnse_2012, hassler_majorana_2014, seleznev_density_2025}, suppression and induction of superconductivity. Said effects are applicable in the field of superconducting spintronics \cite{linder_superconducting_2015, leksin_peculiarities_2013, leksin_boosting_2016, kamashev_superconducting_2019, gordeeva_record_2020, heim_ferromagnetic_2013}. Given that the proximity effect shifts the critical temperature of the phase transition, it can be used for designing superconducting spin valves. In particular, by controlling e.g. the magnetization of the second material, the superconductor can be switched between the superconducting and normal states, thus opening and closing the valve. This motivates the current study, as one is interested in the properties of the critical temperature of the superconducting layer, when designing the superconducting spin valve, as well as the spatial distributions of the order parameter and the anomalous Green function in the superconductor.

The dirty superconductor is described by two equations: the Usadel equation \cite{usadel_generalized_1970} and the self--consistency equation \cite{tinkham_introduction_2015}. The first one defines the spatial distribution of the normal and anomalous Green functions in the superconductor. The second one defines the spatial distribution of the order parameter and the critical temperature of the phase transition. Together they form a system of coupled nonlinear differential equation and integral equation. Its numerical solution requires computationally expensive iterative procedure \cite{tumanov_2024}. However, if the temperature is close to the critical temperature, the Usadel equation can be linearised and becomes a second order linear equation. It still remains coupled to the integral equation for the order parameter, but extensive analytical study can be performed, which is the goal of our study. 

In the present manuscript we find the formal solution of the coupled linear Usadel equation and the self--consistency equation by expressing the solution of the Usadel equation in the integral form, substituting it in the self--consistency equation and decoupling two equations. Now decoupled integral equation for the order parameter is formally solved and the resolvent is explicitly constructed. The critical temperature of the superconductor is defined by condition of existence of the solution of an eigenvalue problem, the corresponding matrix is also explicitly found. In general case, the eigenvalue problem has to be solved numerically, however this is significantly simpler, than performing a usual iterative procedure. Finally, the solution defines the spatial distributions of the anomalous Green function and the order parameter in the superconductor.


\section{Statement of the problem}
We consider a finite--sized superconductor of length $l$ in the dirty limit, brought in contact with some other material such as another superconductor, insulator, magnetic, etc. The goal is to find the critical temperature $T_c$ of the superconducting phase transition, the spatial distributions of the anomalous Green function and order parameter. The order parameter and the critical temperature are connected via the self--consistency equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:SC}
\Delta(x) = U \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} \Re f_s(x) \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) d \varepsilon.
\end{equation}
Here $f_s(x)$ is the anomalous Green function and for the dirty superconductor at $T = T_c$ it obeys the linearised Usadel equation \cite{buzdin_proximity_2005}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Usadel}
\begin{aligned}
&f_s''(x) -k^2(\varepsilon) f_s(x) = \frac{\Delta(x)}{i D}\\
&k^2(\varepsilon) = \frac{2 i \varepsilon}{D}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The proximity effect, induced via the contact with another materials at the border, defines the boundary conditions \cite{zaitsev_quasiclassical_1984, kurpianov_influence_1988, linder_quasiclassical_2022, eschrig_general_2015}. We consider the linearised boundary conditions in form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BC}
\begin{aligned}
&f_s'(0) = \alpha_0 f_s(0) &f_s'(l) = \alpha_l f_s(l).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The coefficients $\alpha_{0,l}$ are defined by the exact type of the materials in contact with the superconductor. So, from a mathematical point of view, we must solve a system of coupled integral and differential equations. 

\section{Formal solution}
\subsection{Solution of the Usadel equations}
We start by expressing the function $f_s(x)$ as an integral of $\Delta(x)$ with some integral kernel. This is done by applying a usual Green function technique of solving nonhomogeneous differential equations \cite{duffy_greens_2001}. In particular,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:f_sol}
f_s(x) = c_+(\varepsilon) e^{k(\varepsilon) x} + c_-(\varepsilon) e^{-k(\varepsilon) x} + \frac{1}{i D}\int\limits_0^l H(x, x'; \varepsilon) \Delta(x') dx'.
\end{equation}
Here $H(x, x'; \varepsilon)$ is the Green function of the differential operator $\partial_x^2 - k^2(\varepsilon)$ with homogeneous boundary conditions, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\partial_x^2 H(x, x'; \varepsilon) - k^2(\varepsilon) H(x, x'; \varepsilon) = \delta(x - x')\\
&\partial_x H(x, x'; \varepsilon) \Big|_{x = 0} = \partial_x H(x, x'; \varepsilon) \Big|_{x = l} = 0.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The function $H(x, x'; \varepsilon)$ will be explicitly constructed in sec. \ref{sec:H}, for now we continue with the general form (\ref{eq:f_sol}) of the solution of the Usadel equation.

Next, from the boundary conditions (\ref{eq:BC}) one finds the coefficients $c_\pm (\varepsilon)$, which turn out to be in form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cpm}
c_\pm (\varepsilon) = \int\limits_0^l \lambda_\pm(x', \varepsilon) \Delta(x') dx'.
\end{equation}
Explicit expression for $\lambda_\pm(x', \varepsilon)$ are written down in Appendix \ref{sec:app}, see eq. (\ref{eq:lambda}). Finally, the solution of the Usadel equation is brought in form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fs_H}
f_s(x) = \int\limits_0^l \left\{\lambda_+(x', \varepsilon) e^{k(\varepsilon) x} + \lambda_-(x', \varepsilon) e^{-k(\varepsilon) x} + \frac{H(x, x'; \varepsilon)}{i D} \right\}\Delta(x') d x'.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Solution of the self--consistency equation}
So, in equation (\ref{eq:fs_H}) we have expressed the anomalous Green function as an integral of the order parameter $\Delta(x)$. This expression, when substituted in the self--consistency equation (\ref{eq:SC}), gives an integral equation for the order parameter:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Delta_int_eq}
\begin{aligned}
\Delta(x) &= U \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) d \varepsilon \times\\
&\times \int\limits_0^l \Re\left\{\lambda_+(x', \varepsilon) e^{k(\varepsilon) x} + \lambda_-(x', \varepsilon) e^{-k(\varepsilon) x} + \frac{H(x, x'; \varepsilon)}{i D} \right\} \Delta(x') dx'.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
This is a homogeneous Fredholm integral equation of the second kind. It can be understood as an eigenvalue problem for an integral operator with corresponding kernel: the order parameter $\Delta(x)$ is an eigenfunction and $T_c$ is defined by setting a corresponding determinant equal to zero. 

Note, that the $w_\pm(x', \varepsilon) e^{\pm kx}$ terms are separable, so the integration over $x'$ can be formally carried out. This eliminates the order parameter from the corresponding terms, in particular
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gamma_def}
\begin{aligned}
&\int\limits_0^{\omega_D} \tanh\left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) d\varepsilon \int\limits_0^l \lambda_\pm(x', \varepsilon) e^{\pm k(\varepsilon) x} \Delta(x') d x' = \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} e^{\pm k(\varepsilon) x}  \tanh\left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) \gamma_\pm(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon\\
&\gamma_\pm(\varepsilon) = \int\limits_0^l w_\pm(x', \varepsilon) \Delta(x') dx'.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The coefficients $\gamma_\pm (\varepsilon)$ are unknown at this point and we will have to determine them later. The self--consistency equation now becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Delta_int_eq_2}
\begin{aligned}
&\Delta(x) = \int\limits_0^l G(x, x') \Delta(x') d x' + v(x)\\
&G(x, x') = U \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} \Re \left\{ \frac{H(x, x'; \varepsilon)}{i D} \right\} \tanh\left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) d \varepsilon\\
& v(x) = U \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} \Re \left\{\gamma_+(\varepsilon) e^{k(\varepsilon) x} + \gamma_-(\varepsilon) e^{-k(\varepsilon) x} \right\} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right)d \varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
This a nonhomogenous Fredholm integral equation of the second kind, not a homogeneous one as in case of (\ref{eq:Delta_int_eq}). These types of equations have formal solutions  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:self_cons_sol}
\Delta(x) = \int\limits_0^l R(x, x') v(x') dx',
\end{equation}
where $R(x, x')$ is the so--called resolvent kernel \cite{polyanin_handbook_1998}. Our next goal is to construct the resolvent kernel and determine $\gamma_\pm(\varepsilon)$, which will complete the solution.

\section{Construction of the resolvent}
The resolvent kernel $R(x, x')$ is the kernel of the linear operator $\hat R$, which is inverse to the operator $1 - \hat G_0$, where $\hat G_0$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
\hat G_0 y(x) = \int\limits_0^l G_0(x, x') y(x') dx'.
\end{equation}
If we are able to perform a spectral expansion of $\hat G$, i.e. find its eigenvalues $g_n$ and eigenfunctions $\psi_n(x)$, such that
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\hat G = \sum g_n |\psi_n \rangle \langle \psi_n| &G(x, x') = \sum_{n = 0}^\infty g_n \psi_n(x) \psi_n(x'),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
we immediately find for the resolvent
\begin{equation}\label{eq:R_def}
\begin{aligned}
&\hat R = (1 - \hat G_0)^{-1} = \sum_{n = 0}^\infty \frac{|\psi_n \rangle \langle \psi_n|}{1 - g_n} &R(x, x') = \sum_{n = 0}^\infty \frac{\psi_n(x) \psi_n(x')}{1 - g_n}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Spectral expansion of $\hat G$ and construction of $\hat R$}\label{sec:H}
In order to find the spectral expansion of $\hat G$ we first expand the integral operator $\hat H$ with kernel $H(x, x'; \varepsilon)$ and use the definition (\ref{eq:Delta_int_eq_2}) of $G(x, x')$. Given that $H(x, x'; \varepsilon)$ is the Green function of the Usadel equation (\ref{eq:Usadel}) with homogeneous boundary conditions, we can represent it as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:H0_eigen}
H(x, x'; \varepsilon) = \sum_{n = 0}^\infty \frac{\psi_n(x) \psi_n(x')}{h_n(\varepsilon)}.
\end{equation}
Here $\psi_n(x)$ and $\lambda_n(\varepsilon)$ are solutions of the equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\psi''_n(x) - k^2(\varepsilon) \psi_n(x) = h_n(\varepsilon) \psi_n(x)\\
&\psi'_n(0) = \psi'_n(l) = 0.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
From this we find 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:psi}
\begin{aligned}
&\psi_n(x) = \cos \left(\frac{\pi n x}{l} \right)\\
&h_n(\varepsilon) = - k^2(\varepsilon) - \frac{\pi^2 n^2}{l^2} = - \frac{2 i \varepsilon}{D}  - \frac{\pi^2 n^2}{l^2}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Next, substituting in (\ref{eq:H0_eigen}) and according to definition (\ref{eq:Delta_int_eq_2}) of $G(x, x')$ we obtain the spectral expansion
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&G(x, x') = \sum_{n = 0}^\infty g_n \psi_n(x) \psi_n(x')\\
&g_n = \frac{U}{D} \int \limits_0^{\omega_D} \Re \left\{\frac{1}{i h_n(\varepsilon)}\right\} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2 T_c} \right) d \varepsilon = U \int \limits_0^{\omega_D} \frac{2 l^4 \varepsilon}{4 l^4 \varepsilon^2 + \pi^4 n^4 D^2} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2 T_c} \right) d \varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Finally, according to (\ref{eq:R_def}) the spectral expansion of the resolvent kernel is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:R_expans}
R(x, x') = \sum_{n = 0}^\infty \frac{\psi_n(x) \psi_n(x')}{1 - g_n}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{The leading contribution from the bulk superconductor}
It is noteworthy, that in the zeroth order
\begin{equation}
G(x, x') \approx U \int \limits_0^{\omega_D} \frac{1}{2\varepsilon} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2 T_c} \right) d \varepsilon
\end{equation}
and the homogeneous self--consistency equation becomes
\begin{equation}
\Delta(x) \approx  U \Delta(x) \int \limits_0^{\omega_D} \frac{1}{2\varepsilon} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2 T_c} \right) d \varepsilon,
\end{equation}
which is the standard result of the BCS theory \cite{tinkham_introduction_2015}. The higher order terms in the expansion thus can be thought as corrections due contributions from the borders of the superconductor.


\section{Eigenvalue problem for $\gamma_\pm(\varepsilon)$ and $T_c$}
In order to complete the solution (\ref{eq:self_cons_sol}) we have to determine unknown coefficients $\gamma_\pm(\varepsilon)$ in the solution. The equations for $\gamma_\pm(\varepsilon)$ are obtained from their definition (\ref{eq:gamma_def}) in which we substitute $\Delta(x)$ given by (\ref{eq:self_cons_sol}). This leads to a system of two integral equations, the solution of which defines $\gamma_\pm(\varepsilon)$:
\begin{equation}
\gamma_\pm(\xi) = \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2 T_c} \right) d \varepsilon \int\limits_0^l dx \int\limits_0^l dx' w_\pm(x, \xi) R(x, x') \Re \left\{\gamma_+(\varepsilon) e^{k(\varepsilon) x'} + \gamma_-(\varepsilon) e^{-k(\varepsilon) x'} \right\}.
\end{equation}
This is again an eigenvalue problem for an integral operator. We will rewrite it as an eigenvalue problem for a matrix, which will make it more suitable for analytical study and numerical solution, and lead to a more concise equation defining $T_c$. We write the real part as $\Re(z) = (z + z^*)/2$, formally integrate with respect to $x$, $x'$ and obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gamma_eigen}
\begin{aligned}
\gamma_\pm(\xi) =  \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} &\Big\{A_{\pm,+}(\xi, \varepsilon) \gamma_+(\varepsilon) +  A_{\pm,-}(\xi, \varepsilon) \gamma_-(\varepsilon) + B_{\pm,+}(\xi, \varepsilon) \gamma^*_+(\varepsilon) + B_{\pm,-}(\xi, \varepsilon) \gamma^*_-(\varepsilon) \Big\}d\varepsilon\\
&A_{\pm, j}(\xi, \varepsilon) = \frac{1}{2} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) \int\limits_0^l dx \int\limits_0^l dx' \lambda_j(x, \xi) R(x, x') e^{\pm k(\varepsilon) x'}\\
&B_{\pm, j}(\xi, \varepsilon) = \frac{1}{2} \tanh \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{2T_c} \right) \int\limits_0^l dx \int\limits_0^l dx' \lambda_j(x, \xi) R(x, x') e^{\pm k^*(\varepsilon) x'}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Integral kernels $A_{\pm, j}(\xi,\varepsilon)$ and $B_{\pm, j}(\xi,\varepsilon)$ can be expressed as sums using the expansion (\ref{eq:R_expans}) of the resolvent kernel, for details see Appendix \ref{sec:app}. We also note, that $B_{\pm, j} (\xi, \varepsilon) = - A_{\pm, j} (\xi, -\varepsilon)$, but will stick to current notation for convenience. 

This system of equations can be written in matrix form if we introduce vector notation $\gamma(\xi) = (\gamma_+(\xi)\ \ \gamma_-(\xi))^T$ and linear operators
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\hat A_{ij} \gamma_\pm(\xi) = \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} A_{ij}(\xi, \varepsilon) \gamma_\pm(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon
&\hat B_{ij} \gamma_\pm(\xi) = \int\limits_0^{\omega_D} B_{ij}(\xi, \varepsilon) \gamma_\pm(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
This allows to rewrite (\ref{eq:gamma_eigen}) in the matrix form as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gamma_mat}
\begin{aligned}
&\gamma = A \gamma + B \gamma^*\\
A &= \begin{pmatrix}
\hat A_{++} & \hat A_{+-}\\
\hat A_{-+} & \hat A_{--}
\end{pmatrix}\ \ \
B = \begin{pmatrix}
\hat B_{++} & \hat B_{+-}\\
\hat B_{-+} & \hat B_{--}
\end{pmatrix}. 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The conjugate $\gamma^*$ is found from the conjugate equation $\gamma^* = A^* \gamma^* + B^* \gamma$ and substituting it back in (\ref{eq:gamma_mat}) we end up with an equation
\begin{equation}
\gamma = A \gamma + B(1 - A^*)^{-1} B^* \gamma.
\end{equation}
The critical temperature $T_c$ is such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Tc_det}
\operatorname{det}\left[A + B (1 - A^*)^{-1} B^* - 1\right] = 0.
\end{equation}
Also one could find the vector $\gamma$, which in essence is the eigenvector of the matrix $A + B (1 - A^*)^{-1} B^*$, corresponding to the eigenvalue $1$, and substitute values of $\gamma_\pm(\xi)$ in the solution (\ref{eq:self_cons_sol}) of the self--consistency equation. This will result in the spatial distribution of the order parameter in the superconductor.

\section{Discussion}
We have analytically solved the coupled linearised Usadel equation and the self--consistency equation as much as it is possible in the general case, without defining specific coefficients in the boundary conditions (\ref{eq:BC}). The solution of the self--consistency equation is expressed via the resolvent, which is explicitly found and presented as a series, which allows, in principle, to find corrections to the zeroth order solution. The latter turns out to be the BCS theory homogeneous solution, as expected.

The critical temperature of the superconducting phase transition is defined by the condition of existence of the solution of a certain eigenvalue problem. The expression for the corresponding matrix is also found explicitly. The eigenvalue problem might be solved numerically in the general case and also further analytical study is possible, if one fixes particular $\alpha_{0,l}$ in (\ref{eq:BC}) and restricts himself with some finite order of expansion of the resolvent. The numerical solution of the obtained eigenvalue problem is also a step forward from the existing in the literature iterative procedure, which is much more computationally complicated. Although the latter allows to find solutions at any temperature, it is excessive if one is interested only in the critical temperature and the corresponding solution.

